WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.876
<v The Announcer>Previously, on LAiDIES: our heroine got
a gorgeous, confident answer from the

00:00:05.876 --> 00:00:12.280
<v The Announcer>machine — and caught the one quiet line
in it that was completely, confidently
wrong.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:18.740
<v The Announcer>She learned that before her name goes on
anything, she checks it like Elle Woods.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:24.837
<v The Announcer>And on this episode: three weeks in, she
realizes she's been talking to this

00:00:24.837 --> 00:00:30.220
<v The Announcer>thing every single day... and has no
idea where it came from, or what it even
is.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:36.540
<v The Announcer>So she goes looking for the origin story
— and finds out it was women all along.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.200
<v The Announcer>This is Episode Four: The Founding
Mothers.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.821
<v The Heroine>It's a slow Wednesday, and I'm
mid-sentence with the machine — asking

00:00:45.821 --> 00:00:51.168
<v The Heroine>it something, correcting it, the whole
married-couple energy we've got going

00:00:51.168 --> 00:00:54.640
<v The Heroine>now — when it hits me, and I actually
stop typing.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.400
<v The Heroine>I have been talking to this thing every
day for three weeks.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.620
<v The Heroine>I've briefed it, argued with it, caught
it lying to my face.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.820
<v The Heroine>And I could not tell you the first thing
about where it came from.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.820
<v The Heroine>What it even is. Who made it.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:21.980
<v The Heroine>It's like I moved in with someone and
realized I'd never once asked to meet
their family.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:29.032
<v The Heroine>And I couldn't help but wonder... this
thing that showed up and rearranged my

00:01:29.032 --> 00:01:31.520
<v The Heroine>whole workweek — is it actually new?

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:40.340
<v The Heroine>And the question underneath that one,
the one I almost didn't let myself ask:
who built it?

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:46.071
<v The Heroine>Welcome back to LAiDIES — smart, busy
women learning AI one Wednesday at a

00:01:46.071 --> 00:01:49.240
<v The Heroine>time, from a little internet town called
SUNNYVAiLE.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.280
<v The Heroine>Three weeks ago you stopped feeling
behind.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.540
<v The Heroine>Then you learned to brief it like a new
hire.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.820
<v The Heroine>Last week, to fact-check it like a
lawyer.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.660
<v The Heroine>So you can genuinely use the thing now.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.140
<v The Heroine>But I'd learned to drive the car without
ever once asking who built the engine.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.980
<v The Heroine>So this week, no new trick. This week's
a flashback.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.900
<v The Heroine>It actually started with a coworker
saying it.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.968
<v The Heroine>"Just use AI for that." As if "AI" were
a complete instruction — and not an

00:02:23.968 --> 00:02:31.140
<v The Heroine>entire mall directory of tools all
wearing the same name tag. Which AI?
ChatGPT?

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:36.960
<v The Heroine>Claude? The one my company announced in
a town hall that nobody can even find?

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.020
<v The Heroine>Because "just use AI" isn't really an
instruction.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.480
<v The Heroine>"AI" is the name of a whole field.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:51.140
<v The Heroine>It's like being told to "just use
internet." ...Use internet for what?

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.520
<v The Heroine>With the internet, you already know
which part does what — you don't even
think about it.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.420
<v The Heroine>With AI, nobody has handed you that map
yet.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.440
<v The Heroine>And the coworker who told me to "just
use it"?

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.580
<v The Heroine>She couldn't have told me either.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:14.440
<v The Heroine>But before I could even get to which
part does what, I got stuck on a much
more basic question.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:22.480
<v The Heroine>So this week: what is this thing —
really — and how did it get all the way
to your desk.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:29.480
<v The Heroine>And for that, I went up the hill, to the
LUMINAiRY — SUNNYVAiLE's hall of heroes.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:35.060
<v The Heroine>Everybody knows the front room: the
patron saints, the Chers and the Elles.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.780
<v The Heroine>But there's a quieter wing in the back
I'd never once walked into.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.900
<v The Heroine>No movie stars in there. ...Well. Almost
no movie stars.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:48.840
<v The Heroine>Just the women who actually built the
thing this whole town is about.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:58.190
<v The Heroine>They call them the MAiVENS. And if you
sit down in that wing and ask "so, how
did we get here," they will

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:04.800
<v The Heroine>tell you the entire story. The lights go
soft. Somewhere, a harp.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:09.380
<v The Heroine>Stay with me — we're going back. It
starts in the eighteen-forties.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:16.125
<v The Heroine>With a young woman staring at a giant
mechanical calculator — a machine that

00:04:16.125 --> 00:04:20.400
<v The Heroine>could do exactly one thing: crunch
numbers. Let her tell you what she saw.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.780
<v Ada Lovelace>They built it to do arithmetic.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.580
<v Ada Lovelace>And everyone who looked at it saw...
arithmetic.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.460
<v Ada Lovelace>A very expensive adding machine.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:33.180
<v Ada Lovelace>I looked at it and saw something else
entirely.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:38.590
<v Ada Lovelace>Because I understood: if a machine can
follow instructions, and you can write

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:43.300
<v Ada Lovelace>those instructions down precisely
enough, then numbers are only the
beginning.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.280
<v Ada Lovelace>It could work with symbols. It could set
them to music.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.660
<v Ada Lovelace>It could make things no one had made
before.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.880
<v Ada Lovelace>So I wrote the instructions down — a
method, step by step, for the machine to
follow.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:05.440
<v Ada Lovelace>The first of its kind. Though — mind you
— I also said plainly: it has no
pretensions to originate anything.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:11.580
<v Ada Lovelace>It only ever does what we know how to
order it to do. Remember that part.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.020
<v Ada Lovelace>Everyone forgets that part.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.060
<v The Heroine>That's Ada Lovelace.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:24.990
<v The Heroine>Eighteen forty-three — before the
lightbulb. She wrote the first algorithm
for a machine that wouldn't be built for

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:30.260
<v The Heroine>another century, and in the same breath
told us exactly what it could and
couldn't be.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:35.756
<v The Heroine>And for the next hundred years, they
handed the credit to the man whose

00:05:35.756 --> 00:05:40.740
<v The Heroine>machine it was — and quietly decided a
woman couldn't possibly have done the
math.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.880
<v The Heroine>Now jump ahead a century, to nineteen
forty-two.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:48.400
<v The Heroine>And this next one — you already know her
face.

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:54.960
<v The Heroine>She was billed, at the time, as the most
beautiful woman in the world. Movie
star.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:00.360
<v The Heroine>Bombshell. And between takes, she was
inventing.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:04.300
<v Hedy Lamarr>Everyone assumed there was nothing
behind the face.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:11.240
<v Hedy Lamarr>It is a very useful thing, to be
underestimated — no one watches what
you're actually doing.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:16.917
<v Hedy Lamarr>There was a war on, and the
radio-controlled torpedoes kept getting

00:06:16.917 --> 00:06:20.140
<v Hedy Lamarr>jammed — the signal was too easy to find
and block.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:26.976
<v Hedy Lamarr>So a friend and I designed a system that
hops: the signal leaps from frequency to

00:06:26.976 --> 00:06:31.984
<v Hedy Lamarr>frequency, dozens of them, too fast to
catch — and the receiver hops right

00:06:31.984 --> 00:06:33.520
<v Hedy Lamarr>along with it, in step.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.120
<v Hedy Lamarr>You cannot jam a signal you cannot find.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.320
<v The Heroine>Hedy Lamarr — with the composer George
Antheil.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:46.940
<v The Heroine>The Navy said no — too bulky, they said
— and shelved it.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:53.560
<v The Heroine>But that idea, a signal hopping to stay
hidden, is in the family tree of the

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:59.513
<v The Heroine>whole wireless world you're standing in:
the Wi-Fi, the Bluetooth, the invisible

00:06:59.513 --> 00:07:02.600
<v The Heroine>hum that carries everything to
everywhere.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:09.600
<v The Heroine>She never made a dime from it, and no
one called her an inventor until she was
nearly gone.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:16.460
<v The Heroine>The most beautiful woman in the world —
and nobody heard a single word she said.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:23.775
<v The Heroine>And three years later — nineteen
forty-five — with the war still on, the

00:07:23.775 --> 00:07:29.808
<v The Heroine>Army in Philadelphia switches on one of
the first true electronic computers: a

00:07:29.808 --> 00:07:36.300
<v The Heroine>thirty-ton, room-sized machine called
the ENIAC, built to calculate artillery
tables.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:40.520
<v The Heroine>But a machine that new doesn't come with
instructions.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.540
<v The Heroine>Somebody has to teach it, physically,
what to do.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:51.120
<v The Heroine>And the Army hands that job — the part
they figure is the tedious part — to six
women.

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:56.680
<v The Heroine>Jean, Betty, Kay, Marlyn, Ruth, and
Frances.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:04.335
<v The Heroine>With no manual and no programming
language to write in — because one did

00:08:04.335 --> 00:08:10.385
<v The Heroine>not exist yet — they program the thing
by hand: rewiring it, cable by cable,

00:08:10.385 --> 00:08:16.120
<v The Heroine>switch by switch, working out how to
make a machine follow a plan at all.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:20.880
<v The Heroine>They are, quite literally, the first
programmers.

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:27.140
<v The Heroine>And when the ENIAC is shown to the
press, the men in the photographs get
named.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:33.960
<v The Heroine>The six women — standing right there, at
the machine they programmed — do not.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:39.750
<v The Heroine>For decades, people who saw those
pictures simply assumed they were

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.760
<v The Heroine>models, posed to make the equipment look
good.

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:49.180
<v The Heroine>It took about forty years for anyone to
go back and learn who they actually
were.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:52.500
<v The Heroine>But that reckoning comes much later.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:09:00.120
<v The Heroine>For now — the machines are about to leap
ahead, and they bring a brand-new
headache with them.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:01.320
<v The Heroine>Nineteen fifty-two.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.500
<v The Heroine>The machines are real now — but talking
to one is agony.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:13.507
<v The Heroine>You had to write in raw code, the
machine's own language, every

00:09:13.507 --> 00:09:15.040
<v The Heroine>instruction by hand.

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:22.280
<v The Heroine>Enter a mathematician the Navy almost
didn't take — too old, they said, at
thirty-six.

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:28.860
<v The Heroine>Too small. They took her anyway. Thank
goodness.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:37.020
<v Grace Hopper>They kept telling me a computer could
only handle numbers — that it could
never understand words.

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:40.080
<v Grace Hopper>I found that a failure of imagination.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:43.860
<v Grace Hopper>Why should a person have to learn to
think like a machine?

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:48.780
<v Grace Hopper>Backwards, the whole thing. So I built a
translator — a compiler.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:53.886
<v Grace Hopper>You write what you want in something
close to plain English, and it does the

00:09:53.886 --> 00:09:55.560
<v Grace Hopper>converting into code for you.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:58.340
<v Grace Hopper>I taught the machine to meet us halfway.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:04.480
<v Grace Hopper>Every app you tap, every chatbot you
open, sits on top of that one idea.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:10.140
<v The Heroine>Grace Hopper. She'd make Rear Admiral;
they named a warship after her.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:16.576
<v The Heroine>And once, when an actual moth flew into
the machine and jammed it, her team

00:10:16.576 --> 00:10:21.784
<v The Heroine>taped the thing into the logbook —
"first actual case of a bug being

00:10:21.784 --> 00:10:27.240
<v The Heroine>found." That is where "debugging" comes
from. She kept the logbook.

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:34.460
<v The Heroine>And it's right here — nineteen fifty-six
— that the men finally show up and give
it a name.

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:40.306
<v The Heroine>A handful of them get a room at
Dartmouth for the summer, write up a

00:10:40.306 --> 00:10:45.900
<v The Heroine>proposal, and christen the whole dream:
"artificial intelligence." Names on it

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:47.600
<v The Heroine>as the founding fathers.

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:54.140
<v The Heroine>And then they promise the world it'll be
basically solved... by the end of the
summer.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.140
<v The Heroine>It was not solved by the end of the
summer.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:00.740
<v The Heroine>It wasn't solved for decades.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:06.234
<v The Heroine>The funding dried up, the promises
curdled, and "AI" became a slightly

00:11:06.234 --> 00:11:08.440
<v The Heroine>embarrassing thing to say out loud.

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:13.420
<v The Heroine>They call those the AI winters. Roughly
two of them.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:16.080
<v The Heroine>It got cold more than once.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:19.920
<v The Heroine>But even in the cold, the work didn't
stop.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:27.131
<v The Heroine>Nineteen seventy-two — a woman at
Cambridge cracks a problem that sounds

00:11:27.131 --> 00:11:31.580
<v The Heroine>tiny and turns out to be enormous: how
does a machine find the right thing?

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:39.740
<v Karen Spärck Jones>Hand a machine a mountain of documents
and ask it to find what matters, and

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:43.980
<v Karen Spärck Jones>here is the trick: the common words tell
you nothing.

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:49.900
<v Karen Spärck Jones>"The." "And." "Is." Useless.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:54.120
<v Karen Spärck Jones>It's the rare words that carry the
meaning.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:00.454
<v Karen Spärck Jones>So I built a way to weigh them — to let
the machine value the word that's

00:12:00.454 --> 00:12:03.380
<v Karen Spärck Jones>unusual over the word that's everywhere.

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:10.000
<v Karen Spärck Jones>It is the arithmetic underneath every
search box you have ever typed into.

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:16.065
<v Karen Spärck Jones>And underneath the modern ones, too —
the systems that go and look things up

00:12:16.065 --> 00:12:18.080
<v Karen Spärck Jones>before they dare to answer you.

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:20.460
<v The Heroine>Karen Spärck Jones.

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:26.010
<v The Heroine>She spent most of her career on short
contracts — no permanent post until

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:30.700
<v The Heroine>nineteen ninety-three, which she said
plainly was because she was a woman.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.160
<v The Heroine>Usually the only one in the room.

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:40.740
<v The Heroine>And she had a line she liked to repeat,
that I want carved over the door of this
entire episode:

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:44.420
<v Karen Spärck Jones>Computing is too important to be left to
men.

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:53.140
<v The Heroine>...Write that one down. And then —
decades later, after almost everyone had
given up — the thaw.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:13:00.040
<v The Heroine>It comes from a professor at Stanford
named Fei-Fei Li, and it comes from a
heretical idea.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:03.820
<v The Heroine>Everyone was busy trying to build a
smarter brain.

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:11.080
<v The Heroine>She said the brain was never the problem
— the problem was we'd never given it
enough to look at.

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:16.182
<v The Heroine>She'd come to this country at sixteen,
barely speaking English, working

00:13:16.182 --> 00:13:18.620
<v The Heroine>weekends in her family's dry-cleaning
shop.

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:24.859
<v The Heroine>And she builds the thing everyone told
her was far too big to bother with: a

00:13:24.859 --> 00:13:30.352
<v The Heroine>collection of millions upon millions of
labeled images, so a machine could

00:13:30.352 --> 00:13:33.940
<v The Heroine>finally learn what the world actually
looks like.

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:40.380
<v The Heroine>And in twenty-twelve, a program trained
on her pictures suddenly sees —

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:45.400
<v The Heroine>recognizes images better than anything
before it, by a landslide.

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:54.960
<v The Heroine>That is the spark. That "AI boom" people
won't stop talking about starts right
there, with her data.

00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:02.880
<v The Heroine>The world took to calling her the
Godmother of AI. Godmother. Not
godfather.

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:06.440
<v The Heroine>And after that, it moves fast.

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:12.518
<v The Heroine>Twenty-seventeen, a team at Google
publishes a new design that finally

00:14:12.518 --> 00:14:17.540
<v The Heroine>cracks how a machine handles language —
and for the first time, the thing can
really write.

00:14:18.860 --> 00:14:23.983
<v The Heroine>And then, one perfectly ordinary
Wednesday — November, twenty-twenty-two

00:14:23.983 --> 00:14:30.580
<v The Heroine>— someone wraps all of it in a little
chat box, puts it online for free, and
calls it ChatGPT.

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:37.814
<v The Heroine>By one widely-cited estimate, a hundred
million people were using it within

00:14:37.814 --> 00:14:41.800
<v The Heroine>about two months — the fastest anything
had ever caught on.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:47.620
<v The Heroine>That's the day AI stopped being a lab
thing and became a you thing.

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:53.016
<v The Heroine>The day it landed on your desk — the
very desk you were sitting at three

00:14:53.016 --> 00:14:54.660
<v The Heroine>weeks ago, feeling behind.

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:58.820
<v The Heroine>But here's the part the highlight reel
skips.

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:04.660
<v The Heroine>As it got powerful, another set of women
got loud — on purpose.

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:12.248
<v The Heroine>Joy Buolamwini, a grad student at MIT,
goes to use face-detection software...

00:15:12.248 --> 00:15:15.440
<v The Heroine>and it can't see her. Her dark skin.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:19.740
<v The Heroine>Not until she literally holds up a white
mask.

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:25.507
<v The Heroine>So she proves it: tests the big
commercial systems and shows they fail

00:15:25.507 --> 00:15:28.180
<v The Heroine>hardest of all on darker-skinned women.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:35.737
<v The Heroine>Timnit Gebru — whose own doctoral
advisor was Fei-Fei Li — teamed up with

00:15:35.737 --> 00:15:40.411
<v The Heroine>a linguist, Emily Bender, to warn that
these language machines can sound

00:15:40.411 --> 00:15:44.700
<v The Heroine>brilliant while understanding nothing,
and to name what that costs.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:51.708
<v The Heroine>Bender gave the problem a name that
stuck: a stochastic parrot — a bird that

00:15:51.708 --> 00:15:57.020
<v The Heroine>mimics speech perfectly, with not the
faintest idea what it's saying.

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:03.930
<v The Heroine>Gebru raised it inside Google, and in
twenty-twenty she was abruptly gone —

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:08.180
<v The Heroine>she says she was fired; Google says it
accepted a resignation.

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:14.800
<v The Heroine>What is not in dispute: thousands of her
colleagues signed their names in
protest.

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:22.472
<v The Heroine>And Kate Crawford maps the part nobody
wants to look at — that behind the

00:16:22.472 --> 00:16:29.000
<v The Heroine>"magic" is a supply chain of mines,
water, electricity, and underpaid human
hands.

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:34.880
<v The Heroine>Her line: AI is neither artificial nor
intelligent.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.140
<v The Heroine>These are not the buzzkills at the
party.

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:42.780
<v The Heroine>They are the reason the thing you're
about to trust is worth trusting.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:46.560
<v The Heroine>Last week you learned to check the
machine.

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:50.140
<v The Heroine>These are the women who check the whole
machine.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:57.900
<v The Heroine>And then the lights come up, and it's
just me, in the back of the LUMINAiRY,
holding all of it.

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:05.520
<v The Heroine>Almost two hundred years. A room full of
women, most of whom had to fight to be
believed.

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:08.820
<v The Heroine>One very confident chatbot.

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:15.080
<v The Heroine>And THAT is what's been sitting quietly
in your browser tab this whole time.

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:19.320
<v The Heroine>Not magic. Not born last Tuesday.

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:26.760
<v The Heroine>The newest chapter, the one still being
written this very minute: agentic AI —

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:31.940
<v The Heroine>the kind that doesn't just answer you,
it goes and does the thing.

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.960
<v The Heroine>That's the edge of the map right now.

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:41.293
<v The Heroine>So — the cocktail-party version, for
when someone at happy hour asks, "is

00:17:41.293 --> 00:17:47.028
<v The Heroine>this whole AI thing brand new, or what?"
It's almost two hundred years old and

00:17:47.028 --> 00:17:49.860
<v The Heroine>about three years old at the same time.

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:55.520
<v The Heroine>The science has been building for
centuries, your access to it is brand
new.

00:17:56.440 --> 00:18:02.114
<v The Heroine>And every real leap in it — the idea,
the signal, the language, the finding,

00:18:02.114 --> 00:18:04.840
<v The Heroine>the sight — has a woman's name on it.

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:09.920
<v The Heroine>Whether or not the textbook bothered to
write it down. And that's the episode.

00:18:10.900 --> 00:18:13.600
<v The Heroine>No try-on task this week — a field trip.

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:16.920
<v The Heroine>Everything you need lives at laidies.ai.

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:23.560
<v The Heroine>That's "LAiDIES" with an i in the
middle: L, A, i, D, I, E, S.

00:18:24.460 --> 00:18:29.152
<v The Heroine>And if you'd rather read than listen,
the whole story's written up there too —

00:18:29.152 --> 00:18:32.240
<v The Heroine>every name and date where you can check
it yourself.

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:37.800
<v The Heroine>And here's the thing — I only had time
to introduce you to a handful of
MAiVENS.

00:18:38.800 --> 00:18:44.080
<v The Heroine>The wing is full of them, more women
than any one episode could ever hold.

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:49.791
<v The Heroine>So your assignment: go up to the
LUMINAiRY, meet one MAiVEN I didn't even

00:18:49.791 --> 00:18:53.660
<v The Heroine>name today, and text one friend a single
sentence about her.

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:59.700
<v The Heroine>Because it's very hard to feel behind on
something the moment you find out it

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:04.800
<v The Heroine>took almost two hundred years and a
hundred brilliant women to hand it to
you.

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:11.028
<v The Heroine>Then, if you're feeling it: grab this
week's study pack at the Blend and Snap,

00:19:11.028 --> 00:19:16.343
<v The Heroine>take the pop quiz at SUNNYVAiLE High —
banking butterfly clips in your closet —

00:19:16.343 --> 00:19:20.860
<v The Heroine>and turn on K-S-V-L, ninety-nine point
nine, for this week's anthem.

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:27.440
<v The Heroine>And if this is your first Wednesday with
us, stop by Makeover on Main for your
residence card.

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:31.940
<v The Heroine>Two minutes, free, and everything you
collect starts counting.

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:36.580
<v The Heroine>So remember, LAiDIES: you were never
behind on AI.

00:19:37.700 --> 00:19:44.109
<v The Heroine>You were just never told it was yours —
built by women, for almost two hundred

00:19:44.109 --> 00:19:47.760
<v The Heroine>years, until it finally knocked on your
door.

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:51.800
<v The Heroine>Computing is too important to be left to
men.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:55.400
<v The Heroine>See you next Wednesday... in SUNNYVAiLE.

00:19:56.800 --> 00:20:02.425
<v The Announcer>Next time on LAiDIES: our heroine opens
the AI her company installed... and it

00:20:02.425 --> 00:20:05.700
<v The Announcer>answers exactly like the one she uses at
home.

00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:12.178
<v The Announcer>She learns the difference between the
brand on the door and the brains behind

00:20:12.178 --> 00:20:16.960
<v The Announcer>it — and why "which AI is best" was the
wrong question all along.

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:21.240
<v The Announcer>Come back next week for Episode Five:
The Super Models.
